WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
Capability discovery is the process by which an MCP client learns what a server offers.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:12.740
At a high level, the client can discover available tools, resources, or prompts and make those capabilities available to the host application.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.360
Discovery helps avoid hard-coding every integration into the AI application.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:26.460
It also creates a governance question: learning that a capability exists does not mean the capability should be trusted, enabled, or used in every context.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.640
A typical interaction flow starts with a user request.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.940
The AI application sends the relevant prompt and context to the model.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.440
The model may reason over the request and decide that additional context or a tool call would help.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:46.940
The host may present available capabilities, enforce policy, ask for user approval, or call a tool through the MCP client.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.240
The server executes the requested operation or returns resource content.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.220
The tool result then becomes part of the context for the final response.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.680
Traceability matters throughout that flow.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:04.060
Context and tool results should remain connected to their source so users and operators can tell where information came from.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.160
If a model summarizes a document, the application should know which document was used.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:14.980
If a tool returned a status, the application should know which server and downstream system produced it.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.320
Traceability supports user trust, troubleshooting, auditing, and incident response.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.940
Discovery should be paired with selection rules.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:31.400
A server may expose many capabilities, but a given user, workspace, tenant, or task should only see the capabilities that policy allows.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.540
Some tools may require approval.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.400
Some resources may be filtered by user identity.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.100
Some capabilities may be disabled in production.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:47.080
MCP gives teams a structured interaction model, but the host application still needs to decide what context and actions are appropriate for the current request.
